{
  "0": "Interaction mechanism: replace dot-product compatibility with a jointly trained diagonal bilinear score, s(h,i)=sum_d a_d*h_d*e_i,d, initializing a to ones. Learned coordinate weights may suppress noisy compatibility dimensions without adding substantial cost. Preserve the GRU, embeddings, history readout, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer to reproduce scoring. Train one model per category across all four datasets.",
  "1": "Embedding representation: cap each nonpadding item embedding's effective L2 norm at 1 using e/max(1,||e||_2), consistently wherever that embedding table is consumed. This may limit item-norm dominance and improve generalization in sparse categories. Preserve embedding dimensions, scoring, GRU, history policy, loss and optimizer; mirror the parameterization in the predictor writer. Retain one model for each of the four datasets.",
  "2": "Training strategy: multiply the current learning rate by 0.75, leaving optimizer type, batch size, scheduler, dropout and weight decay unchanged. Smaller updates may reduce the validation oscillation and early overfitting seen in Electronics while retaining gradual learning in CDs_and_Vinyl. Preserve architecture, loss, sampling, seeds, validation selection, stopping rules, epoch cap 200 and resource allocation. Apply to all four category models.",
  "3": "Learning objective: replace the current loss with candidate-set cross-entropy, -s_positive+logsumexp(s_positive,s_negative_1,...), using exactly the existing sampled negatives and valid-position mask. Direct competition among candidates may improve top-10 ordering more than the previously unsuccessful small pairwise auxiliary term. Preserve embeddings, GRU, scoring/readout, optimizer, sampling and training settings. Train one independent model for each of the four datasets."
}
